{:edb/db-before-t 1279 :edb/db-after-t 1280 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534592 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:51:55.334Z" 13194139534592 true] [17592186078149 1100 "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/620/Source/Images/038c730db46c27977ecaf5b36ce200bc.png)" 13194139534592 false] [17592186078149 1100 "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/e03fb046f74771676d7b1e83a1cf09176adcffaf8112e9de263404b8be74b026.png)" 13194139534592 true] [17592186078149 1145 "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm}$, its radius is\n\n$$\nr =6\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d r}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2) \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\pi r^2, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)= 4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$.\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"." 13194139534592 false] [17592186078149 1145 "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm},$ its radius is $r =6\\, \\text{cm}.$ Now, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$  is given by \n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$  with respect to $r$ is \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\nV'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d r}\\left(  \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right)\\[5pt]\n &  =  \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2)\\[5pt]\n &= 4\\pi r^2, \n}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)=  4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}.$\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"." 13194139534592 true] [17592186078150 1100 "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p$, in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" 13194139534592 false] [17592186078150 1100 "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p,$ in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" 13194139534592 true] [17592186078150 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5)$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(b) &= \\frac{d}{db}(5e^{-0.2b}) \\\\\n&= 5e^{-0.2b} \\cdot (-0.2) \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(5) &= -e^{-0.2(5)} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{e}\n$$\n\ndollars per pound." 13194139534592 false] [17592186078150 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(b) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(5{e}^{-0.2b}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-0.2b}\\cdot (-0.2) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.2b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(5) & =-{e}^{-0.2(5)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is $-\\dfrac{1}{e}$ dollars per pound." 13194139534592 true] [17592186078251 1100 "A particle moves in a straight line. After $t$ seconds the particle is $\\, f(t)=2t^3 - 5t + 9\\,$ feet away from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position as a function of $t$?" 13194139534592 false] [17592186078251 1100 "A particle moves in a straight line. After $t$ seconds the particle is $\\, f(t)=2t^3 - 5t + 9\\,$ feet away from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position as a function of $t?$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186078251 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is equal to $f'(t)$.\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 5t + 9) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t$ seconds, the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is $6t^2 - 5$ feet per second." 13194139534592 false] [17592186078251 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is equal to $f'(t).$ \n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-5t+9) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t$ seconds, the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is $6t^2 - 5$ feet per second." 13194139534592 true] [17592186078306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297627 13194139534592 true] [17592186078306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297638 13194139534592 true] [17592186078307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297645 13194139534592 true] [17592186078307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297659 13194139534592 true] [17592186078408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297620 13194139534592 true] [17592186078408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297624 13194139534592 true] [17592186078408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297652 13194139534592 true] [17592186078408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297666 13194139534592 true] [17592186259051 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274589 13194139534592 false] [17592186259051 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297614 13194139534592 true] [17592186259051 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference." 13194139534592 false] [17592186259051 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference." 13194139534592 true] [17592186259051 1145 "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534592 false] [17592186259051 1145 "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186260569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274750 13194139534592 false] [17592186260569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297632 13194139534592 true] [17592186260569 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{'}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day." 13194139534592 false] [17592186260569 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{′}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day." 13194139534592 true] [17592186266166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186266166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953428/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139534592 false] [17592186266167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297631 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297615 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297616 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297617 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297618 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297619 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1041 "selection" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297617 13194139534592 true] [17592186297614 1110 #uuid "316d79eb-c7b6-5349-bbfa-8d92c0c4eeba" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297615 1034 "\\frac{C}{4\\pi }" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297615 1111 #uuid "838cf9ad-6de2-55f4-a99c-8657f56e70ba" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297616 1034 "C" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297616 1111 #uuid "8c248248-84d5-57d4-b6ce-fe4a85f98373" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297617 1034 "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297617 1111 #uuid "7fcbd2af-f496-5ae2-aff8-1c1a8c03dcfd" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297618 1034 "\\frac{2C}{\\pi }" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297618 1111 #uuid "2e8e9cc9-632f-59bf-914f-6bcbd2efd7d5" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297619 1034 "C\\pi " 13194139534592 true] [17592186297619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297619 1111 #uuid "e78e8e43-abd8-5cf1-8c86-fd1003f57849" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297620 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297621 13194139534592 true] [17592186297620 1089 "q-343166" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534592 true] [17592186297620 1100 "The funding $F,$ in millions of dollars, allocated to a research project $t$ years after it begins is modeled by\n\n$$\nF(t)=5+(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the funding as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the funding is\n{{field-1}} million dollars per year." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297620 1113 #uuid "2e07b371-489a-5311-9833-37b80a53596c" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297620 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the funding is ${F}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[5+(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+(t+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{t+1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the funding is $1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ million dollars per year." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297622 13194139534592 true] [17592186297621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297623 13194139534592 true] [17592186297621 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534592 true] [17592186297621 1041 "field-1" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297621 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297622 13194139534592 true] [17592186297621 1110 #uuid "12f72ab5-e1eb-5d08-8081-32ef946fbe1c" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297622 1034 "\\ln(t+1)+1" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297622 1111 #uuid "9537d5e3-26d5-5698-96a6-c53109fc8e5b" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297623 1034 "\\ln(t+1)" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297623 1111 #uuid "b6934440-20a2-590b-a245-66e7f0a746d2" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297624 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297625 13194139534592 true] [17592186297624 1089 "q-343167" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297624 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534592 true] [17592186297624 1100 "The inventory level $I,$ in hundreds of units, of a medical supply $t$ weeks after ordering begins, is modeled by\n\n$$\nI(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level as a function of $t?$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is\n{{field-1}} hundreds of units per week." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297624 1113 #uuid "3771af58-f6ea-5a0e-8fb6-883a58c8196f" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297624 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is ${I}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12] \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}+{t}^{2}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}(2-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is $t{e}^{-t}(2-t)$ hundreds of units per week." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297626 13194139534592 true] [17592186297625 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534592 true] [17592186297625 1041 "field-1" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297625 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297626 13194139534592 true] [17592186297625 1110 #uuid "956351f3-e5a9-506b-b875-9603998a6bb0" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297626 1034 "2te^{-t}-t^2e^{-t}" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297626 1111 #uuid "9b79824f-0d58-561b-a13e-7f5242c46a7c" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297627 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297628 13194139534592 true] [17592186297627 1089 "q-343498" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297627 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534592 true] [17592186297627 1100 "The surface area $A$ of a sphere is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing with respect to its radius at the moment when the radius of the sphere is equal to $5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its radius is $5$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297627 1113 #uuid "507d7e12-bec8-5924-a111-bf667b784658" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297627 1145 "We know that the surface area of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(r)=4\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =8\\pi r,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(5)=8\\pi (5)=40\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing when its radius is $5\\,\\text{in}$, is $40\\pi $ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297628 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297629 13194139534592 true] [17592186297628 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297630 13194139534592 true] [17592186297628 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534592 true] [17592186297628 1041 "field-1" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297628 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297629 13194139534592 true] [17592186297628 1110 #uuid "13cf4b10-a598-5e0e-921f-502564520d45" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297629 1034 "40\\pi" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297629 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297629 1111 #uuid "c9d523ba-9b37-5749-9a36-a7981cff36a4" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297630 1034 "100\\pi" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297630 1111 #uuid "8bc9dae0-08fc-5a43-9634-6a5ab6bd891b" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297631 1034 "1000" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297631 1111 #uuid "c48d34f1-d84d-51c7-96b0-c287ce05a6d3" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297633 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297634 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297635 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297636 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297637 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1041 "selection" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297634 13194139534592 true] [17592186297632 1110 #uuid "b314822c-8502-5d9c-96db-55ae51a7bb14" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297633 1034 "$1\\,695$ computers per day" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297633 1111 #uuid "9302bc57-0b92-57ac-ac8a-40346871ae5f" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297634 1034 "$1\\,699$ computers per day" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297634 1111 #uuid "b02029e3-c2e5-5cae-8e1e-987d224a052d" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297635 1034 "$1\\,495$ computers per day" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297635 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297635 1111 #uuid "dc73284c-f8d5-5ff3-b9bd-96d45f3598be" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297636 1034 "$1\\,501$ computers per day" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297636 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297636 1111 #uuid "53d48ccb-79d3-5295-9847-af807c0197c6" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297637 1034 "$1\\,801$ computers per day" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297637 1111 #uuid "e0cc9bae-b4a6-56f2-8dd2-682f8c8008c6" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297638 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297639 13194139534592 true] [17592186297638 1089 "q-48728" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297638 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534592 true] [17592186297638 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/d56c10dfa022454135ddafd4255f737182bdca186b99ed1e686132447341bff8.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $20\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297638 1113 #uuid "0340e2b4-a8dc-5b2e-8637-96bee21fbe98" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297638 1145 "Since we are given that the diameter of the spherical balloon is $20\\,\\text{cm}$,  its radius $r$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a sphere with radius $r$  is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(r)=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3{r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi {r}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=4\\pi (10)^{2}=400\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is increasing when the diameter is $20\\,\\text{cm}$ is $400\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297640 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297641 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297642 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297643 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297644 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1041 "selection" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297640 13194139534592 true] [17592186297639 1110 #uuid "b395a869-7c8e-5438-8347-1bdf9c83021e" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297640 1034 "400\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297640 1111 #uuid "b3b19f6b-d5db-5615-bdec-a86c99773854" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297641 1034 "1\\,600\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297641 1111 #uuid "0c2cc91f-1d24-59d8-8e2f-34d800d495e6" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297642 1034 "300\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297642 1111 #uuid "f2443660-7bd3-5940-b5e3-362e8ac4ce51" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297643 1034 "400\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297643 1111 #uuid "6404aa9b-f9e2-5e42-b886-2a1142be79e6" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297644 1034 "300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297644 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534592 true] [17592186297644 1111 #uuid "ed587da6-042b-5ee8-852e-c07fb7353538" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297645 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297646 13194139534592 true] [17592186297645 1089 "q-72356" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297645 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534592 true] [17592186297645 1100 "The temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, of a rapidly cooling substance after $t$ minutes can be modeled by the function $F(t)=250-{t}^{3}.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing with respect to time at the moment when the temperature of the substance is equal to $34$ degrees Fahrenheit." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297645 1113 #uuid "af3a3dc0-c338-5ee0-b1a3-cea63f01a521" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297645 1145 "Since we are given that the temperature of the substance is $34$ degrees Fahrenheit, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & =250-{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =216 \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing when $t=6$ minutes is equal to ${F}^{′}(6).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $F(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250-{t}^{3})=-3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=6$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(6)=-3(6)^{2}=-108.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing when $t=6$ minutes is $-108$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297647 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297648 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297649 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297650 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297651 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1041 "selection" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297649 13194139534592 true] [17592186297646 1110 #uuid "bad2a0a6-bc28-5701-b465-2fb9a735e0eb" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297647 1034 "$-50$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297647 1111 #uuid "469086ff-5fd5-54a7-bfc0-faecc7f5c7e8" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297648 1034 "$-72$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297648 1111 #uuid "566cf014-59dd-57f5-b3a0-7b04511b2e46" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297649 1034 "$-108$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297649 1111 #uuid "0381cefa-c2aa-56e1-8f37-1d69d183b280" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297650 1034 "$-54$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297650 1111 #uuid "02baa62e-d511-5d50-846b-133b571bc3b2" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297651 1034 "$-36$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297651 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297651 1111 #uuid "3deab803-29e9-5aac-b603-3c7f8f038bef" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297652 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297653 13194139534592 true] [17592186297652 1089 "q-72357" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297652 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534592 true] [17592186297652 1100 "The air temperature $F$ in degrees Fahrenheit can be modeled by the function $F(C)=\\frac{9}{5}C+32,$ where $C$ is the air temperature in degrees Celsius. Find the instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297652 1113 #uuid "60bc5656-b32e-5701-901a-41c0c8fd54d3" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297652 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is equal to ${F}^{′}(C).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $F(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{9}{5}C+32) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is $\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius." 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297654 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297655 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297656 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297657 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297658 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1041 "selection" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297655 13194139534592 true] [17592186297653 1110 #uuid "8b62f26e-2cfb-5faf-8c53-54f20df0dca0" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297654 1034 "$\\frac{169}{5}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297654 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297654 1111 #uuid "43329b9d-e113-5c0f-a41e-cfeead79d7b8" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297655 1034 "$\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297655 1111 #uuid "46ff578c-cac4-563d-8412-e9062ca9639f" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297656 1034 "$\\frac{5}{169}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" 13194139534592 true] [17592186297656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534592 true] [17592186297656 1111 #uuid "1c71d900-2229-5eea-a385-492c5d4668dc" 13194139534592 true] [17592186